# Acer AN515-54 GTX1650 i7 - SREP Bootable USB Setup

This repo prepares a bootable UEFI USB drive for the Acer AN515-54 that automatically launches the Smokeless Runtime EFI Patcher (SREP) when the machine boots.

The project is built around the upstream SREP project:
https://github.com/Maxinator500/SmokelessRuntimeEFIPatcher-RUS

## Why this repo exists

The upstream SREP project is powerful but it requires manual setup and a firmware-friendly config. This repo automates the USB boot workflow and gives you a sane starting point for Acer AN515-54 use.

It includes:
- USB creation scripts
- UEFI `startup.nsh` auto-launch
- a sample SREP config file named for Acer AN515-54
- BIOS boot checklist
- safety guidance

## Important warning

SREP can modify firmware at runtime. A bad patch can leave a machine unbootable.

- Keep a BIOS backup before trying patches
- Start with logging and minimal config changes
- Use the included sample config only as a template
- Disable Secure Boot before booting from USB
- Do not flash random unknown byte patterns into your BIOS

## What you need

- A USB stick (8GB+ recommended)
- Windows, Linux, or macOS
- The actual `SREP.efi` binary from the upstream repo
- An Acer AN515-54 laptop

## Quick start

### 1) Download SREP

You can either:
- download the official release from the upstream repo, or
- run the included download script

Linux/macOS:
```bash
bash download_srep.sh
```

Windows:
```powershell
download_srep.bat
```

This will try to fetch the latest `SREP.efi` from GitHub releases.

### 2) Create the bootable USB

Linux/macOS:
```bash
bash prepare_usb.sh
```

Windows:
```powershell
prepare_usb.bat
```

If you pass a device path manually, the script accepts it too:
```bash
bash prepare_usb.sh /dev/sdb
```

### 3) Boot the USB on Acer AN515-54

1. Insert USB
2. Power on and press F2 or Del during boot
3. Set BIOS Boot Mode to UEFI
4. Disable Secure Boot
5. Save BIOS changes
6. Boot from USB (F12 / Esc depending on model)
7. The UEFI shell should auto-execute `startup.nsh`
8. `startup.nsh` will look for `SREP.efi` and the config file

